&lt;h2 id="setup"&gt;Setup&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Data.&lt;/strong&gt; TinyStories — ~2M short stories generated by GPT-3.5/4, designed so a small model can learn coherent English.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Discovery Needs Dense Rewards?</title><link>https://anuragprat1k.github.io/posts/wordle-reward-shaping/</link><pubDate>Mon, 23 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://anuragprat1k.github.io/posts/wordle-reward-shaping/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Fine-tuning a language model with RLVR is known to &lt;strong&gt;sharpen&lt;/strong&gt; it — the policy concentrates on known solutions, boosting pass@1 while pass@k collapses (&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13837"&gt;Yue et al., 2025&lt;/a&gt;; &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07912"&gt;Zhao et al., 2025&lt;/a&gt;). This is increasingly treated as a fundamental property of RL on language models. But it shouldn&amp;rsquo;t be. Sharpening is what happens when binary rewards provide zero gradient on problems the model can&amp;rsquo;t already solve — the capability frontier never moves because the optimization has nothing to push against.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Process Rewards: An Overview and Outlook</title><link>https://anuragprat1k.github.io/posts/process-rewards/</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 May 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://anuragprat1k.github.io/posts/process-rewards/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="1-introduction"&gt;1. Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Enhancing LLMs in complex domains requiring multi-step reasoning, logical consistency, and adherence to procedural constraints remains a critical research challenge. Process Reward Models (PRMs) offer the promise to address some of these limitations by providing fine-grained supervision signals throughout the model&amp;rsquo;s generation process, both at inference and training. They offer denser credit assignment than Outcome Reward Models (ORMs), but are hard to annotate and often ambiguous since characteristics of a good process vary by domain and difficulty of the problem at hand.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>